# Managing the Mac Studio

## Overview

The Mac Studio is a headless home server that is reached from this MacBook over
Tailscale SSH. Run every remote command through the bundled helper. Plain `ssh`
runs into four problems here:

| Problem with plain ssh | What the helper does |
|---|---|
| The login user is `__SERVER_USER__`, not the local `chandima` | Sets the user |
| `ssh __SERVER_NAME__` doesn't resolve | Uses `__SERVER_IP__` |
| Tailscale check mode can demand a browser sign-in, and `BatchMode` hides the link | Prints the login URL and waits for approval |
| Tailscale SSH always exits 0 | Returns the real remote exit code |
| A non-interactive shell has no Homebrew/Docker on PATH | Adds `/opt/homebrew/bin` to PATH |

```bash
bash ~/.claude/skills/__SERVER_NAME__/scripts/studio.sh health         # status of everything
bash ~/.claude/skills/__SERVER_NAME__/scripts/studio.sh run '<cmd>'    # run any command there
bash ~/.claude/skills/__SERVER_NAME__/scripts/studio.sh copy <src> <dst>
```

**If the helper prints a `login.tailscale.com` URL**, give it to the user. They
must open it in the browser profile signed in to the tailnet (`__TAILNET_ACCOUNT__`), not
their default profile. The helper waits up to 5 minutes. Run the helper in the
foreground, not in the background, so the URL reaches you.

## Machine

- __SERVER_SPECS__
- Tailscale address: `__SERVER_IP__`, MagicDNS `__SERVER_NAME__.__TAILNET__`

## Services

| Service | Endpoint | How it runs | Manage |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Splash** (Qwen3.8-27B LLM) | `http://__SERVER_IP__:8000` (OpenAI `/v1/chat/completions` and Anthropic `/v1/messages`) | Foreground `splash serve` in a login shell, **not a service** | see below |
| **SearXNG** + Valkey | `http://__SERVER_NAME__.__TAILNET__:8889` (`/search?q=…&format=json`) | Docker Compose on Colima, published with `tailscale serve` | `~/searxng-stack/searxng.sh up\|down\|restart\|status\|logs\|test` |
| **image-server** (Qwen-Image 2.1 via mflux) | `http://__SERVER_NAME__.__TAILNET__:8890` (`POST /v1/images/generations`, OpenAI-style) | LaunchAgent `com.searxng-stack.image-server`; model loads on demand | `~/searxng-stack/image-server/image-server.sh install\|status\|logs\|restart\|unload\|test` |
| **Colima** (Docker VM: 2 CPU, 2 GB) | — | `brew services` LaunchAgent, starts at login | `colima status\|start\|stop` |
| **Ollama** | `127.0.0.1:11434`, local to the Studio only | macOS app | — |

The SearXNG stack is a git checkout of `github.com/chan4lk/searxng-stack`. To
update it: `cd ~/searxng-stack && git pull && ./searxng.sh restart`. Its `.env`
holds the instance secret and is git-ignored, so never commit it.

### Splash

Splash serves **one model at a time**. Both of these are downloaded:

| Model | Size on disk |
|---|---|
| `incoai/Qwen3.8-27B-Splash` | 16 GB, dense |
| `incoai/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Splash` | 20 GB, mixture of experts |

A request that names the model that isn't loaded gets `HTTP 404 model_not_found`.

It isn't supervised by anything: it doesn't survive a reboot, and killing it
takes the model offline for every client, including the MacBook's
`claude-splash` and `dsh-splash`. **Ask the user before stopping, restarting or
switching Splash's model.**

Check what's loaded and how it was started:

```bash
studio.sh run 'ps -o command= -p $(lsof -t -iTCP:8000 -sTCP:LISTEN)'
```

**`--host` matters.** Splash defaults to `127.0.0.1`, which the MacBook can't
reach. It must run with `--host __SERVER_NAME__` to be reachable over the tailnet.
To start it or switch model (`MODEL` is one of the two above):

```bash
studio.sh run 'pkill -f "splash serve|libexec/server/server.py"; sleep 3; nohup splash serve --host __SERVER_NAME__ --port 8000 --model MODEL > ~/splash.log 2>&1 &'
```

Loading the model takes a while, so check `curl -s http://__SERVER_IP__:8000/v1/models` until it lists the model.

### image-server

When idle it uses about 100 MB. The first request loads the model, which takes
a minute or more. It unloads after 10 idle minutes (`IMAGE_IDLE_UNLOAD`). A
request can pass `"quantize"` as 0 (bf16), 4 or 8; the default is 8. Requests
run one at a time, and after 4 are waiting, new ones get `429`.

| Setting | Per the mflux docs (M5 Max benchmark) |
|---|---|
| bf16 (`quantize` 0) | Peak ~46 GB; ~78 s for 1024², 40 steps |
| 8-bit | Less memory; the 17.5 GB text encoder always stays bf16 |

**Memory with Splash:** bf16 doesn't fit next to Splash. Before a bf16 run,
check free memory, or ask the user to stop Splash first. Free memory
immediately with `image-server.sh unload`. The service writes PNGs to
`~/image-server-outputs` and deletes them after 7 days.

```bash
curl -s http://__SERVER_NAME__.__TAILNET__:8890/v1/images/generations -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"prompt":"...","size":"1024x1024","steps":40,"response_format":"url"}'
```

**From dsh:** `dsh-splash` has a `generate_image` tool, from the plugin
`~/.dsh/plugins/image-generate.mjs`. It saves PNGs into the session's workspace
(default `generated-images/`, or a `filename` relative to the workspace) and
refuses any path outside it. The alias points it at the server with
`IMAGE_SERVER_URL`. The chat UI only draws images on `read_image`'s own card,
so when the current model accepts images, the tool's result tells the agent
to call `read_image` on the saved file. That shows the image in the chat and
lets the model check it. Only the 35B-A3B entry declares image input
(`input: [text, image]` in `splash.settings.yaml`). On the 27B the image is
saved but not shown. If the tool reports that the image server is unreachable,
check `image-server.sh status` on the Studio.

## Common mistakes

- **Deleting or overwriting files, or stopping services, without asking.** The Studio is shared infrastructure, so confirm these first. Read-only checks such as `health`, `status` and `logs` are fine without asking.
- **Trusting `brew services list`.** It can show Colima as `stopped` while the VM is running. Check with `colima status`.
- **Installing packages.** Use `HOMEBREW_NO_AUTO_UPDATE=1 NONINTERACTIVE=1 brew install …`. A `brew install` that waits for input hangs the SSH session.
- **Opening SearXNG to the internet.** Its bot limiter is off because it's reached only from the tailnet. Never bind it to `0.0.0.0` or enable `tailscale funnel`.
